100:Runtime supervision is delegated to `agentwrap`. The platform runtime package must stay generic: it may know about prompts, working directories, models, timeouts, permissions, events, and execution results, but it must not know about studies, dimensions, sources, synthesis gating, report semantics, project catalogs, sprint stages, or product state machines.
101-
102-The CLI must not become the only place where use cases are assembled. Shared application operations should be extracted from command-specific glue into testable structs/functions that CLI commands, TUI actions, and local HTTP handlers can call. Neither TUI nor web code should invoke `ultraplan` subprocesses for normal product behavior.
103-
104-## 4. Design Principles
105-
106-- Keep the domain model independent of CLI parsing.
107-- Keep product use cases independent of CLI parsing, TUI widget state, HTTP transport DTOs, and HTML rendering.
108-- Keep runtime adapters independent of study semantics.
109:- Treat filesystem artifacts as durable product state.
110-- Validate outputs before marking tasks successful.
111-- Make state transitions explicit and testable.
112-- Prefer deterministic fixtures for tests.
113-- Do not require real OpenCode for normal unit tests.
114-- Use agentwrap for runtime execution, retries, validation wrapping, permission policy translation, health/preflight checks, observability, metadata, and OpenCode structured-output adaptation.
115-- Keep UltraPlan-specific study, source, report, project, and sprint behavior outside agentwrap adapters and wrappers.
116-- Avoid global mutable state.
117-- Avoid package cycles.
118-- Make dry-run behavior available before expensive operations.
119-- Keep generated Markdown and YAML readable by humans.
120-- Keep the CLI as the stable automation contract even when a TUI is available.
121-- Keep the filesystem and product-owned run state authoritative when the local server is running.
122-- Run review before smoke by default so deterministic conformance failures block unnecessary live-runtime work.
123-- Keep only `review.md` and `smoke.md` in the sprint root; link detailed smoke evidence from the external harness.
124-- Compute review/smoke verdicts from validated evidence and explicit severity rules, not from runtime exit success or unstructured model prose alone.
125-- Preserve exact requirements and code-context bytes in one shared prompt prefix before stage-specific instructions; downstream agents may still inspect additional live source.
126-- Keep Markdown authoritative initially. Any later search record or knowledge graph is derived and rebuildable; any later SQLite authority is an explicit product-mode decision rather than a web-side shadow store.
