59-## 3. Architecture Overview
60-
61-UltraPlan Go should be module-driven, not global-layer-driven. Product behavior should live with the module that owns the relevant state and workflow. Shared platform packages should be limited to genuinely cross-cutting infrastructure such as configuration, logging, filesystem helpers, and runtime execution.
62-
63-The detailed architecture is defined in [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md). The initial implementation should follow this high-level shape:
64-
65-```text
66-cmd/
67-  ultraplan/
68-    main.go
69-
70-internal/
71-  app/                  # composition root, shared use cases, and interface wiring
72-  tui/                  # local terminal UI over app use cases, added after execute
73-  web/                  # loopback HTTP, Go templates/static assets, JSON, and SSE over app use cases
74-  platform/             # generic infrastructure only
75-    config/
76-    logging/
77-    filesystem/
78-    runtime/
79-    process/            # safe external executable/argv execution for the smoke harness
80-  workspace/            # workspace discovery, paths, and validation
81-  study/                # study lifecycle, prompts, scheduling, validation, reports, state
82-  project/              # project docs, project-index cataloging and validation
83-  sprint/               # planning, execute, review, smoke, prompts, validators, state, and flow through smoke
84-  codeextract/          # citation parsing, file resolution, snippet extraction
85-```
86-
87-Sprint 35 may add a focused execution-control package or package-owned repositories exposed through `internal/app`. The reasoning stage must select that shape from concrete lifecycle and concurrency requirements; this TRD intentionally does not reserve a package name before ownership is proven. In every valid shape, `internal/web` remains a transport adapter and no interface process owns the only authoritative run registry.
